{"reference":"Ezekiel 16","book":"Ezekiel","chapter":16,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A difficult metaphor","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Ezekiel 16 personifies Jerusalem as a wife, using marriage, prostitution, exposure, and punishment imagery to frame the chapter’s accusation. The sexualized rhetoric is central to the argument, and many readers find its portrayal of shame and punishment ethically disturbing.","verse_refs":["Ezekiel 16:1-63"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ezekiel_016_b00eb4f1af0ae3b1ae2fda03"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}